Ideas/points:

- Literal family
	- Ma Joad constantly wants to keep the family together and views it as the most important thing
	- Breakdown of family traditions - e.g. leaving land
	- Compared to Gatsby + From -
		- in Gatsby family is not very important, it is a more individualistic and capitalist novel.
		- From wishes to leave his wife for a much younger and non familial woman and shows little faith to his family
	- Connie's selfishness vs Rose of Sharon's commitment

- Family as in community
	- Obviously very important in the Grapes of Wrath
		- This is Casy's whole thing - how we are all part of one bigger whole.
		- Tom starts believing too.
	- It is both a spiritual and socialist thing.
		- Steinbeck as a socialist author is inherently more likely to focus on community and its importance
	- In Gatsby this is not so much of a thing
		- E.g. random people going to parties and Nick's loneliness at the end
		- All very superficial
	- Ethan From
		- in this sense family matter more to Ethan than literally - he wishes to go to cities and halls of people
	- This kinda thing becomes a tool to fight against the isolation that individualism brings to people
	- Workers are one big family
		- Government camp, Wilson's + Joads, Joads accepting Casey

- How different people contribute
	- How men and women contribute to the family
	- Ma cooking and stuff vs Al knowing about cars
    - Family keeps women as women and men as men
    - **BUT** Ma Joad runs the family and keeps it together

